{"task_id": "HumanEval/145", "completion_tokens": 3205, "reasoning_tokens": null, "reasoning_len": 6403, "reasoning_tail": "ctually, I just want to double-check my digit sum for -12. The digits of 12 are 1 and 2, sum is 3. Yes.\n\nAnd for 11, digits are 1 and 1, sum is 2. Yes.\n\nOK, I'm confident in my code. Let me finalize.\n", "finish_reason": "stop", "content_empty": false, "content_len": 1322, "content": "\n\n```python\ndef order_by_points(nums):\n    \"\"\"\n    Write a function which sorts the given list of integers\n    in ascending order according to the sum of their digits.\n    Note: if there are several items with similar sum of their digits,\n    order them based on their index in original list.\n\n    For example:\n    >>> order_by_points([1, 11, -1, -11, -12]) == [-1, -11, 1, -12, 11]\n    >>> order_by_points([]) == []\n    \"\"\"\n    # Compute the sum of digits for each number\n    # and sort by that sum (stable sort preserves original order for ties)\n    def digit_sum(n):\n        n = abs(n)\n        s = 0\n        while n > 0:\n            s += n % 10\n            n //= 10\n        return s\n\n    # Use a simple stable sort (Python's sort is stable)\n    # We sort by digit sum\n    result = list(nums)\n    # Insertion sort for stability\n    for i in range(len(result)):\n        for j in range(i, 0, -1):\n            if digit_sum(result[j]) < digit_sum(result[j - 1]):\n                result[j], result[j - 1] = result[j - 1], result[j]\n            else:\n                break\n    return result\n```\n\nThis function computes the sum of the digits of the absolute value of each integer and sorts the list in ascending order of that sum. Python's sort is stable, so elements with equal digit sums retain their original relative order.", "has_separate_reasoning_field": true, "has_inline_think_tag": false, "wall_s": 139.0, "requested_extra_body": {"chat_template_kwargs": {"reasoning_effort": "medium"}}, "resolved_reasoning_effort": "medium", "resolved_reasoning_effort_source": "requested"}
